# LeakSanitizer suppressions.
#
# Same rule as tests/tsan.supp: keep this file SHORT and every entry justified.
# A suppression is a claim that a reported leak is not real, and an unjustified
# one hides the bugs the sanitizer exists to find. Prefer fixing the code every
# time it is our code.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenSSL provider and DRBG state orphaned by OPENSSL_thread_stop
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reported as: ~7.5 KB in ~40 CRYPTO_zalloc allocations reached through
# EVP_RAND_fetch / ossl_method_construct, in any test binary where a thread pool
# worker has run and exited.
#
# It is OpenSSL's own one-time provider registry and seed DRBG, allocated on the
# first RAND_bytes and normally reachable for the life of the process. It
# becomes UNREACHABLE — and therefore reported — because BoundedThreadPool::run
# calls OPENSSL_thread_stop() as each worker exits, which is correct and
# deliberate: without it, every worker's per-thread DRBG and error queue would
# live until the process ended, and that IS a real leak for a pool that recycles
# threads (src/core/thread_pools.cc). Releasing the per-thread state takes some
# of OpenSSL's shared bookkeeping out of its registry with it.
#
# Evidence it is not ours:
#   * Every frame in every one of the reported stacks is inside libcrypto; the
#     only anvil frame is the RAND_bytes call site.
#   * The amount is fixed. It does not grow with the number of hashes, the
#     number of pool threads, or the number of test cases — a leak proportional
#     to work would not be suppressible on these grounds and is not what this is.
#   * A binary that links no pool at all reports none of it, with identical
#     calls into the same Argon2 and RAND_bytes paths.
#
# The alternative to suppressing it is dropping OPENSSL_thread_stop, which
# trades a bounded one-time report for an unbounded real leak. Revisit if
# OpenSSL ever frees its provider registry on the last thread_stop, and delete
# this entry then.
leak:CRYPTO_zalloc
leak:CRYPTO_malloc
